SCOPE — what this does and does NOT establish (read before quoting):
  ESTABLISHED: identical results under two INSTRUCTION SET ARCHITECTURES
    (arm64 native, x86_64 codegen) and four optimization levels (-O0..-O3).
    This exercises the axes that actually threaten an integer engine:
    instruction selection, integer promotion/narrowing, and optimizer
    reassociation. VDF128_T4 has NO floating-point and NO libm dependency in
    the delay loop (integer + LUT + clean-room SHA-256), so the classic
    libm-divergence failure mode of the Float64 path cannot arise here.
  NOT ESTABLISHED: a native Linux/glibc run on physical x86_64 hardware, and a
    second compiler family (GCC). The x86_64 binaries here are Mach-O executed
    through Rosetta 2 translation; the compiler back end is x86_64, the OS is
    not. Big-endian hosts remain explicitly gated by the sidecar's
    MCL_BIG_ENDIAN_ACK guard and are untested.
  STATUS: partial evidence. The remaining Linux/GCC run stays on the pre-publication
    checklist and must not be described as done.
